-- title:
--   Calculus of fibre readings of sections of ι(V)·Γ(X,U)
-- statement:
--   Fix a commutative ring $B_1$, a field $k$, and a $k$-vector space $V$ that also carries a $B_1$-module structure, together with a $B_1$-linear map $\iota \colon V \to B_1$; fix schemes and morphisms $f \colon X \to \operatorname{Spec} B_1$, $f_k \colon X_k \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and $i \colon X_k \to X$ (no compatibility between $f$, $f_k$ and $i$ is assumed), an open $U \subseteq X$, an open $W \subseteq X_k$ and a proof that $W \le i^{-1}U$. Here $\Gamma(X,U)$ is a $B_1$-algebra through $f$ and $\Gamma(X_k,W)$ a $k$-algebra through $f_k$, and $\delta \in \Gamma(X,U)$ *reads as* a $k$-linear $w \colon V^\vee \to \Gamma(X_k,W)$ (`IsFibreReading`) when there are $n$, $v \colon \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to V$ and $s \colon \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to \Gamma(X,U)$ with $\sum_j \iota(v_j)\, s_j = \delta$ in $\Gamma(X,U)$ and $w(\xi) = \sum_j \xi(v_j)\cdot (i^\sharp_U s_j)|_W$ for every $\xi \in V^\vee$. The conclusion is a conjunction of six statements: $0$ reads as $0$; if $\delta$ reads as $w$ and $\delta'$ as $w'$ then $\delta + \delta'$ reads as $w + w'$; if $\delta$ reads as $w$, $s \in \Gamma(X,U)$ and $w'(\xi) = (i^\sharp_U s)|_W \cdot w(\xi)$ for all $\xi$, then $s\delta$ reads as $w'$; if $\delta$ reads as $w$ then $-\delta$ reads as $-w$; if $\delta$ reads as $w$ on $(U,W)$ and $U' \le U$, $W' \le W$, $W' \le i^{-1}U'$, then $\delta|_{U'}$ reads as the composite of $w$ with restriction $\Gamma(X_k,W) \to \Gamma(X_k,W')$; and every $\delta$ lying in the ideal of $\Gamma(X,U)$ generated by the image of $\operatorname{range}\iota$ under $B_1 \to \Gamma(X,U)$ admits some reading $w$ on $(U,W)$.
--
--   This is the elementary calculus of fibre readings used in the Čech-theoretic analysis of the obstruction to deforming line bundles along a small extension: it records that readings form a module over $\Gamma(X,U)$ compatible with restriction, and that sections of the ideal generated by $\iota(V)$ always have at least one reading. It is invoked wherever readings are assembled locally and compared on overlaps, in particular by the construction of Picard deformation cocycles from Čech trivialisations and by the recognition of invertible pullbacks from such cocycles.
